Dear Sir Roger,

On the occasion of your ninety‑fifth birthday, I am writing from a small 
European Shorthair breeding programme in the Czech Republic, called 
Felis Noetica. We select for temperament, curiosity and problem‑solving 
rather than for appearance; our motto is _Intelligentia et Concordia_ -- 
intelligence and harmony.

On 27 July 2026 a solid black male kitten was born here. On 8 August 
2026 he was named Penrose -- formally _Penrose of Felis Noetica_ -- in 
honour of your work on general relativity and black holes, of the 
Penrose tilings, and of twistor theory.

We name each litter after scientists whose work we admire, and where 
they are living we write to tell them. Please accept the enclosed 
Certificate of Honorary Godparenthood (_Patrinus honorarius_) as a token 
of respect and gratitude for your life's work. We ask nothing in return.

One detail I thought might amuse you. He is the blackest of the four, 
and I cannot photograph him: the autofocus hunts and fails, because too 
little contrast returns from the subject for it to lock onto. Of the 
whole litter, he is the only one I am unable to resolve.

With warmest wishes for your health and happiness, and many happy 
returns,

Yours sincerely,

Viktor Lošťák
Breeder, Felis Noetica
Brno & Runářov, Czech Republic
info@felisnoetica.cz · felisnoetica.cz [1]

